  "theorem_name": "ScaleAdaptiveRadiusCertificateAndSignalPhaseTransition",
  "declared_proposition": "Let C be the nonnegative integer-valued off-critical-pair counting measure of TICKET-217 and L(r)=integral r^t dC(t). For tau>0 set r_H=exp(-tau/H). Then C(H)<=floor(exp(tau)L(r_H)); consequently a rigorous actual-zeta upper bound U_H<exp(-tau) certifies C(H)=0. If such bounds hold on a cofinal sequence of H, then C is identically zero and RH follows within the established defect-measure equivalence. More generally the first-atom signal is r_H^H=exp(-H beta_H), where beta_H=-log r_H. It stays uniformly detectable exactly when H beta_H is bounded above, and vanishes when H beta_H tends to infinity.",
  "mathematical_argument": "TICKET-217 gives C(H)r_H^H<=L(r_H). Substitution of r_H^H=exp(-tau) and integrality gives the certificate. A cofinal zero certificate covers every finite height. The phase statement is the identity r_H^H=exp(-H beta_H): bounded H beta_H gives a positive uniform lower signal, while H beta_H tending to infinity makes that signal tend to zero. This schedule theorem does not construct or bound the actual zeta defect transform.",
